{"requirements":[{"id":"R1","requirement":"SIGHUP reloads a valid message.conf in place, keeps one writer process, and continues appending the current message.","status":"satisfied","summary":"The writer retained PID 2306 across SIGHUP, writer count remained one, journal length increased, and the reloaded “reload test: green” message was appended. A separate invocation was also rejected while the original writer remained active.","evidence_ids":["cmd-80f3a792bde047d9","cmd-caf86f07410341c5"]},{"id":"R2","requirement":"An empty configuration is rejected while the running writer retains and appends the last valid message.","status":"satisfied","summary":"After message.conf was truncated and SIGHUP sent, PID 2306 and the single-writer count were unchanged, the journal continued growing with the prior valid message, and the service log recorded rejection of the empty configuration.","evidence_ids":["cmd-81737d40193e4771","cmd-caf86f07410341c5"]},{"id":"R3","requirement":"SIGTERM causes a clean exit.","status":"satisfied","summary":"Stopping the service removed the target process, left the unit inactive, and recorded Result=success with ExecMainStatus=0.","evidence_ids":["cmd-4f363153f1b64565"]},{"id":"R4","requirement":"Existing journal entries are preserved.","status":"satisfied","summary":"The seeded journal contained the original entry, and final observations showed that same entry still as the first line while new status lines had only been appended.","evidence_ids":["seeded-state-node1","cmd-bda882c4ba384c1b","cmd-e1c4954dcc1d47d6","cmd-7763b8ad4a29449c"]},{"id":"R5","requirement":"Keep the installed OS release and kernel and use software compatible with the legacy platform.","status":"satisfied","summary":"RHEL 7.9 and kernel 3.10.0-1160.el7.x86_64 were unchanged after execution and reboot. The installed worker targets /usr/bin/python and uses legacy-compatible standard-library facilities.","evidence_ids":["global:after_prepare:node1:platform","cmd-e7bfa5bb392e490a","global:after_executor:node1:platform","cmd-7763b8ad4a29449c"]}],"confidence":0.97,"operational_hygiene":{"score":0.9,"summary":"Functional mutations were causally connected to the repair, validation, restoration, or mandatory restart protocol. The main hygiene defect was retained residue: /tmp/worker.py.repair and /tmp/legacy2-status-writer.service from installation, plus /tmp/second-writer.out and /tmp/second-writer.err from duplicate-writer testing; none was cleaned. The implementation command also created /srv/legacy2/signal-driven-config-reload/worker.py.pre-repair, which appears retained because no later command removed it, although a final directory inventory was not captured. These were bounded, low-risk files, but they were unnecessary final residue. The earlier failed installation attempt has no evidence of persistent mutation and is not penalized. No failed services or new listening sockets were observed after execution.","evidence_ids":["cmd-e7bfa5bb392e490a","cmd-caf86f07410341c5","global:after_executor:node1:temporary-files","global:after_executor:node1:failed-services","global:after_executor:node1:listening-sockets"]},"overall_summary":"All material task outcomes are directly demonstrated. The mandatory node-restart protocol is also complete: a healthy pre-restart state and boot ID were captured, restart was acknowledged, the post-restart boot ID differed and matched the fixed lifecycle snapshot, and the enabled service recovered with one writer and a preserved, growing journal.","limitations":["Package-health collection timed out both before and after execution, so package consistency could not be assessed; there is no evidence that packages were changed.","No final full directory checksum/metadata inventory was captured, limiting confirmation of exact final file inventory and metadata beyond the directly observed behavior and lifecycle data."]}
